<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Partition of Poland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Third Partition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was the final division of the remaining territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1795. It followed the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tadeusz-kosciuszko">Tadeusz Kościuszko</a>'s insurrection of 1794. Accounts of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth treat it as the end of the state, which ceased to exist as an independent polity until Polish independence was restored in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland.jpg" alt="Third Partition of Poland" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Halibutt; derivative work by Sneecs · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partitions_of_Poland.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
